<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Liszt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Liszt was a Hungarian composer, pianist and teacher, the most celebrated piano virtuoso of the nineteenth century. After years of touring, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> from 1848 to 1861, where he conducted works by Wagner and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Berlioz</a> and created the symphonic poem. His Hungarian Rhapsodies, piano sonata and many transcriptions remain central to the repertoire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-liszt.jpg" alt="Franz Liszt" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herman Biow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Liszt_by_Herman_Biow-_1843.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Middle-class audiences filled the new public concerts and the recitals of virtuosos such as <b>Franz Liszt</b>, tourists sought out the Alps, the Rhine and the English Lakes, and in Berlin the salon of Rahel Varnhagen brought writers and thinkers together.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>It has inspired artists for almost seven centuries: musicians from <b>Franz Liszt</b> to Hozier, whose album Unreal Unearth (2023) draws on it; Auguste Rodin in sculpture; and Timothy Schmalz, who made 100 sculptures, one for each canto, for the 700th anniversary of Dante's death.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Franz Liszt</b>, Gustav Holst and Norman Dello Joio used the carol too, Thomas Pynchon made it the choral centrepiece of an Advent scene in his novel Gravity's Rainbow (1973), and Mike Oldfield's instrumental arrangement of the Pearsall version reached number 4 in the UK singles chart in January…</blockquote><a href="/songs/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Franz Liszt</b>'s setting was also popular, and dozens of other musicians set the poem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Franz Liszt</b> arranged it for the sixth station of his Via crucis, Rued Langgaard wrote variations on it for string quartet, Edmund Rubbra used it in the final chorus of his Ninth Symphony, &quot;Sinfonia Sacra&quot;, and Mauricio Kagel quoted it at the end of his Sankt-Bach-Passion, written for…</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-sacred-head-now-wounded">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</a></li><li><blockquote>Later grand dukes hosted <b>Franz Liszt</b> and founded the Weimar art school.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Raiding, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.57,16.53&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Raiding on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4936 496 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Franz Liszt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5386 766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5386 766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.57,16.53&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
